  "explanation": "The referenced study describes an assay that detects SARS-CoV-2 directly from saliva and swab samples in 30 minutes [1]. The description does not indicate that the test identifies viral proteins (antigens), only that it detects the virus, so the specific claim about protein identification in 30 minutes is not supported by the provided context [1]. It also reports 97% sensitivity and 100% specificity, without mentioning protein-based detection [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes—the major genetic risk factor for severe COVID-19 is a ~50 kb segment on chromosome 3 inherited from Neanderthals [1]. This Neanderthal-derived segment is associated with increased risk of severe illness and hospitalization and is present at notable frequencies in South Asia (~30%) and Europe (~8%) [1]. However, the provided source does not specify that this inheritance occurred 60,000 years ago, only that it derives from Neanderthals [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes, a large genome-wide association study found that genetic variation is associated with susceptibility to respiratory failure in COVID-19. [1] The study enrolled 1,980 cases across European centers and, after quality control, analyzed 835 Italian cases with 1,255 controls and 775 Spanish cases with 950 controls across 8,582,968 SNPs. [1] Meta-analysis identified genome-wide significant associations at rs11385942 on chromosome 3p21.31 (OR 1.77, 95% CI 1.48–2.11, P=1.14e-10) and rs657152 at 9q34 (OR 1.32, 95% CI 1.20–1.47, P=4.95e-8). [1] The 3p21.31 locus includes SLC6A20, which encodes an interaction partner of ACE2, supporting biological plausibility. [1] The 9q34 signal maps to the ABO blood group locus, with A-positive individuals at higher risk (OR 1.45, 95% CI 1.20–1.75, P=1.48e-4) and blood group O showing a protective effect (OR 0.65, 95% CI 0.53–0.79, P=1.06e-5). [1] The authors concluded these represent robust genetic susceptibility loci for developing respiratory failure in COVID-19. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "According to the referenced study, ultraviolet light was most strongly associated with lower COVID-19 growth rates when modeling early pandemic dynamics using fine-scale weather data and global infection reports [1]. The model explained 36% of the variation in early growth rates before interventions, with 17% attributable to weather or demography, and identified ultraviolet light as the dominant weather factor linked to reduced spread [1]. However, the authors emphasized substantial uncertainty, noting that in the absence of control measures, transmission could remain significant despite seasonal ultraviolet effects [1]. They projected a temporary decrease during summer, followed by a rebound by autumn and a peak next winter, indicating that aggressive policy interventions would still be necessary despite seasonal trends [1]. In particular, they reported that the probability of a weekly doubling rate remained greater than 20% throughout the summer without interventions, underscoring the limits of relying on ultraviolet-related seasonality alone [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "The study estimates that about 1.7 billion people (22% of the global population) have at least one underlying condition that puts them at increased risk of severe COVID-19 if infected [1]. However, the study separately defines a \"high risk\" group—those likely to require hospital admission if infected—and estimates this at about 349 million people (4% of the global population) [1]. Therefore, stating that 1.7 billion people are in the high‑risk category is inaccurate; 1.7 billion refers to increased risk due to underlying conditions, not the high‑risk (hospitalization) category [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "Evidence indicates that bats exhibit a dampened STING-mediated DNA-sensing interferon response rather than an enhanced ability to detect cytosolic DNA. [1] This dampening is associated with a bat-specific replacement of the highly conserved S358 residue in STING, an essential adaptor in multiple DNA-sensing pathways. [1] Reintroducing the conserved S358 residue restored STING functionality, increasing interferon activation and inhibiting viruses, which shows that bats’ native state features reduced DNA sensing/response. [1] Along with bat-specific changes in other DNA sensors (TLR9, IFI16, AIM2), these findings suggest bats adapt to high cytosolic DNA from flight and infections and coexist with viruses by tempering, not amplifying, DNA sensing. [1] Therefore, it is inaccurate to claim that the ability to detect cytosolic DNA per se helps coexistence; rather, a tuned-down DNA-sensing/interferon axis likely contributes to viral tolerance. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Data from China showed that although most COVID-19 cases were mild (81%), approximately 80% of deaths occurred among adults aged 60 years and older, while only one death (0.1%) occurred in a person aged 19 years or younger, indicating substantially lower mortality among children and adolescents compared with older adults [1]. In the United States during February 12–March 16, 2020, 31% of cases, 45% of hospitalizations, 53% of ICU admissions, and 80% of deaths occurred among adults aged 65 years and older, and no ICU admissions or deaths were reported among persons aged 19 years or younger, further demonstrating markedly lower risk of severe outcomes in younger populations [1]. These findings collectively indicate that children under 18 years are at much lower risk of severe symptoms or death from COVID-19 than older adults [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A study of nine human subjects measured cough aerosols (0.35–10 μm) and found averages of 75,400 particles per cough during influenza and 52,200 after recovery, with ranges from 900 to 302,200 when ill and 1,100 to 308,600 after recovery [1]. The average cough aerosol volume was 38.3 picoliters per cough when ill versus 26.4 picoliters after recovery, with a statistically significant increase in volume during illness (p = 0.0143) [1]. On average, 63% of the cough aerosol volume was in the respirable size fraction capable of reaching the alveolar region if inhaled [1]. Although the difference in particle counts per cough between illness and recovery did not reach statistical significance (p = 0.1042), the absolute numbers per cough are substantial [1]. These data demonstrate that humans can expel a large number of respirable aerosol particles by coughing, supporting the statement as true [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "An analysis of 72,314 reported COVID-19 cases in China up to February 11, 2020 found that 80.9% of confirmed cases were classified as mild or mild pneumonia, indicating most had relatively mild illness [1]. Additionally, 889 cases (1.2% of all recorded cases) were asymptomatic, supporting the claim that a notable share had no symptoms [1]. Taken together, these findings support the statement that around 80% of people with reported COVID-19 had relatively mild or no symptoms at that time [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Available modeling of local transmissions up to March 23, 2020 indicates that most outbreaks clustered in relatively cool and dry areas [1]. An ensemble of ecological niche models projected that temperate warm and cold climates are more favorable for SARS-CoV-2 spread, whereas arid and tropical climates are less favorable [1]. These results imply that COVID-19 exhibits greater suitability in cooler and drier conditions, consistent with patterns observed for SARS-CoV-1 [1]. However, the authors note substantial uncertainties across parts of sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America, and Southeast Asia, so this climate niche should be interpreted cautiously [1]. Moreover, climate acts alongside human demography and mobility, with environmental conditions potentially mediating transmission and unsuitable climates destabilizing the virus [1]. If current trends continue, asynchronous seasonal outbreaks could occur globally, further reflecting climate-linked suitability [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Early analyses found that ultraviolet light was most strongly associated with lower COVID-19 growth rates, suggesting a potential summer decrease as UV increases [1]. Using fine-scaled weather and infection data, the authors' model projected that, absent interventions, COVID-19 would decrease temporarily during summer, rebound by autumn, and peak next winter [1]. However, the study emphasized substantial uncertainty, with the model explaining only 36% of variation in early growth rates and only 17% attributable to weather or demography [1]. Importantly, even in summer without control measures, the probability of a weekly doubling rate remained greater than 20%, indicating that reduced growth is not guaranteed [1]. Consequently, the authors concluded that aggressive policy interventions would still be needed despite seasonal trends [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A New York City multicenter cohort reported ischemic strokes in COVID-19 patients spanning ages 25–75 years, with large-vessel occlusion cases being younger (median age 46). [1] About a fifth of patients lacked common stroke risk factors such as diabetes and hypertension, and none had a history of atrial fibrillation or smoking. [1] Additionally, emergent large-vessel occlusion patients had no known cardiac risk factors at presentation, indicating that severe strokes can occur in younger individuals with few preexisting risks. [1] Most patients presented for acute hospitalization due to the stroke, consistent with sudden onset. [1] The authors conclude that COVID-19–related ischemic events may result from hypercoagulability or cardioembolism, mechanisms that can precipitate strokes even in younger patients without traditional risk factors. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes, SARS-CoV-2 can be emitted in respiratory aerosols during vocal activities such as speaking and singing [1]. An investigation of a choir practice found that singing likely contributed to transmission through aerosol emission, resulting in high secondary attack rates among attendees [1]. The report also notes that certain individuals, termed superemitters, release more aerosol particles during speech, which can enhance spread [1]. These observations support public health recommendations to maintain distance and use face coverings to reduce aerosol-mediated transmission [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "In a retrospective case series of 1,591 ICU patients with laboratory-confirmed COVID-19 in Lombardy, Italy, 1,150 of 1,300 patients with respiratory support data (88%) received mechanical ventilation, indicating that a large proportion required mechanical ventilation [1]. Additionally, 1,287 of 1,300 patients (99%) needed some form of respiratory support, underscoring the severity of respiratory failure among these critically ill patients [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Evidence shows COVID-19 patients can be infectious before symptoms appear, with infectiousness peaking on or before symptom onset [1]. In a study analyzing viral shedding in 94 patients and modeling 77 transmission pairs, the highest viral load was observed at the time of symptom onset, supporting presymptomatic infectiousness [1]. The researchers estimated that 44% of secondary cases were infected during the index cases’ presymptomatic stage, indicating substantial presymptomatic transmission [1]. These findings confirm that transmission can occur prior to symptom onset and that control measures should account for this [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A cluster randomized controlled trial of households with an index case of influenza-like illness found that, in intention-to-treat analysis, household contacts of mask-wearing index cases had consistently lower rates of clinical respiratory illness, influenza-like illness, and laboratory-confirmed viral infections compared with controls, although the differences were not statistically significant (e.g., clinical respiratory illness RR 0.61, 95% CI 0.18–2.13) [1]. Because some controls also wore masks, a post hoc as-treated analysis was conducted and showed a protective effect against clinical respiratory illness among contacts of mask-using index cases, but not against influenza-like illness or laboratory-confirmed infections [1]. The authors concluded that medical masks used by sick individuals may provide source control benefits, but the evidence was limited by small sample size and low secondary attack rates, and larger trials are needed to confirm efficacy [1]. Therefore, mask-wearers with clinical respiratory illness appear less likely to transmit clinical respiratory illness to unmasked household members, though the evidence is suggestive rather than definitive [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes, reports from Bergamo province, Italy, documented an outbreak of Kawasaki-like disease in children during the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic, indicating a link between COVID-19 and a Kawasaki-like condition in pediatric patients [1]. The incidence rose from 0.3 cases per month before the epidemic to 10 cases per month after it began, representing about a 30-fold increase [1]. Among the ten children diagnosed during the epidemic period, eight were positive for SARS-CoV-2 IgG or IgM antibodies, consistent with current or prior infection [1]. The affected children were older and showed higher rates of cardiac involvement, Kawasaki disease shock syndrome, and macrophage activation syndrome compared with pre-epidemic cases [1]. Kawasaki-like presentations were managed as Kawasaki disease according to American Heart Association guidance in this cohort [1]. The authors concluded that the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic was associated with a high incidence of a severe form of Kawasaki disease and anticipated similar outbreaks in other affected countries [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Children can become seriously ill from COVID-19, as shown by 48 confirmed pediatric cases admitted to 46 North American pediatric intensive care units between March and April 2020 [1]. Many had significant preexisting comorbidities (83%), indicating vulnerability to severe disease [1]. Severe respiratory involvement was common, with 38% requiring invasive mechanical ventilation [1]. Multiorgan dysfunction occurred, with 23% experiencing failure of two or more organ systems and 2% requiring extracorporeal membrane oxygenation [1]. There were deaths (4%), and some children remained hospitalized requiring ventilatory support at follow-up, confirming that serious outcomes do occur in this age group even though severe illness is less frequent than in adults [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Updated U.S. estimates found that 56.0% of adults reported more than one underlying condition associated with increased risk of severe COVID-19, which logically means more than half have at least one such condition [1]. This update relied on U.S. hospitalization data and replaced cancer with obesity in the condition set, increasing the estimated prevalence of risk-enhancing conditions among adults [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes—the cited retrospective cohort study of 368 hospitalized U.S. veterans with confirmed SARS-CoV-2 found that hydroxychloroquine alone was associated with a higher risk of death compared with no hydroxychloroquine (adjusted hazard ratio 2.61; 95% CI, 1.10 to 6.17; P=0.03) [1]. In that study, mortality rates were 27.8% with hydroxychloroquine alone, 22.1% with hydroxychloroquine plus azithromycin, and 11.4% with no hydroxychloroquine [1]. The authors concluded there was no evidence that hydroxychloroquine, with or without azithromycin, reduced the risk of mechanical ventilation and that hydroxychloroquine alone was associated with increased overall mortality [1]. Because this was a retrospective observational analysis using propensity score adjustment, the findings show association rather than definitive causation and underscore the need for randomized controlled trials [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "An Italian survey of 1,379 health workers involved with the COVID-19 response found severe depression in 24.73% of respondents, anxiety in 19.80%, and insomnia in 8.27%, indicating notable prevalence of these conditions. [1] Being a front-line health worker was associated with poorer mental health outcomes, supporting elevated risk among those treating COVID-19 patients. [1] Overall, the study concluded that a substantial proportion of health workers involved with the COVID-19 pandemic experienced mental health issues. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A large cross-sectional survey of 1,257 health care workers in 34 Chinese hospitals early in the COVID-19 outbreak found high rates of depression (50.4%), anxiety (44.6%), insomnia (34.0%), and distress (71.5%) [1]. Frontline workers directly involved in diagnosis, treatment, and care had significantly higher odds of these symptoms compared with other health care workers: depression OR 1.52, anxiety OR 1.57, insomnia OR 2.97, and distress OR 1.60 [1]. Nurses, women, and those working in Wuhan reported more severe symptoms, further indicating elevated psychological burden among those most exposed [1]. These findings support that frontline health care workers during the coronavirus pandemic suffer elevated rates of mental health issues [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Cats are susceptible to infection with SARS-CoV-2, the virus that causes COVID-19 [1]. The referenced study found that while the virus replicates poorly in dogs, pigs, chickens, and ducks, ferrets and cats are permissive to infection [1]. The researchers also experimentally demonstrated that cats are susceptible to airborne infection by SARS-CoV-2 [1]. These findings indicate that cats can contract COVID-19 and acquire it via respiratory transmission under experimental conditions [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "According to a modeling study of the early Wuhan outbreak, the median basic reproductive number (R0) was estimated to be 5.7 (95% CI 3.8–8.9) when assuming a serial interval of 6–9 days [1]. This analysis also found a rapid early doubling time of 2.3–3.3 days and produced an R0 estimate higher than earlier preliminary estimates of 2.2–2.7 [1]. Therefore, it is accurate to say that during the initial Wuhan outbreak, SARS-CoV-2 was spreading with an estimated median R0 of about 5.7 under those modeling assumptions [1]. This estimate specifically depends on the assumed serial interval used in the analysis (6–9 days) [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "SARS-CoV-2 is transmitted through human respiratory droplets, supporting the statement that COVID-19 may be spread via droplets expelled during routine respiration such as breathing and talking [1]. Measurements in Wuhan hospitals detected SARS-CoV-2 RNA in airborne aerosols and showed increases in areas prone to crowding, which is consistent with the possibility of spread from exhaled droplets and aerosols in shared air [1]. The authors further propose that SARS-CoV-2 may be transmitted through aerosols, although the infectivity of the detected aerosolized virus was not established in this study [1]. While the document does not directly test droplets specifically from breathing or talking, its findings on respiratory droplet transmission and aerosol presence align with that mechanism of spread [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "The cited study found that surgical face masks significantly reduced detection of coronavirus RNA in aerosols exhaled by symptomatic individuals, not in larger respiratory droplets. [1] For coronavirus RNA in respiratory droplets, the study observed only a non-significant trend toward reduction rather than a statistically significant decrease. [1] Therefore, it is not accurate to claim that flat surgical masks significantly reduced coronavirus-carrying droplets released into the surrounding air, although they did significantly reduce coronavirus in aerosols. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "In U.S. data from February 12–March 16, 2020, 45% of hospitalizations, 53% of ICU admissions, and 80% of deaths occurred among adults aged ≥65 years, meaning that the remaining hospitalizations and ICU admissions, as well as 20% of deaths, occurred in people younger than 65, indicating that younger adults also experienced severe outcomes [1]. Additionally, there were no ICU admissions or deaths among persons aged ≤19 years, indicating that severe outcomes observed in those under 65 occurred in adult age groups, not children [1]. While the risk of serious disease and death is higher in older age groups, these findings show that adults across a wide range of ages can become seriously ill from COVID-19 [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A study of 331 hospitalized COVID-19 patients reported that in the severe disease status, female patients tended to have a higher average SARS-CoV-2 IgG antibody level than male patients [1]. The authors further noted that most female patients generated relatively high IgG levels in the severe status, while no sex differences were observed in mild, general, or recovering groups [1]. They also found that antibody generation in females tended to be stronger in the early phase, which they proposed may contribute to different outcomes between genders [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "By mid-March 2020, transmission of SARS-CoV-2 in the United States had accelerated, with rapidly increasing case counts indicating established transmission [1]. Reports beginning in late February of cases without travel links signaled the initiation of pandemic spread, which progressed to accelerated transmission by mid-March, showing that spread speeded up in March 2020 [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "According to a modeled 'shield immunity' intervention, serological testing can identify recovered individuals to be deployed as safer interaction hubs, thereby reducing transmission probability [1]. The model indicates that this approach could substantively reduce the length and overall burden of the outbreak and can work synergistically with social distancing [1]. Therefore, strategically using recovered individuals identified via serology to substitute higher-risk interactions can help reduce the length of the COVID-19 outbreak [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "No—the provided context is a single case report and does not establish that coronavirus complications are higher among patients with heart complications, including those who were previously healthy [1]. It documents an otherwise healthy 53-year-old woman with COVID-19 who developed acute myopericarditis with systolic dysfunction and pericardial effusion, showing that cardiac involvement can occur as a complication even without prior heart disease or respiratory involvement [1]. Because it is an isolated case description without comparative or epidemiologic data, it cannot quantify risk or demonstrate higher complication rates in patients with heart complications [1]. Therefore, while the case illustrates that previously healthy individuals can develop cardiac complications from COVID-19, it does not provide evidence that such patients experience higher overall complication rates [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "High circulating IL-6 levels are associated with increased odds of intubation and predict acute respiratory failure in both COVID-19 and influenza patients [1]. However, the provided study does not present evidence that elevated IL-6 predicts death, so the claim that high IL-6 portends death is not supported by this context [1]. Therefore, the statement is only partially correct with respect to respiratory failure, but not established for death in the provided evidence [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A large cohort study of hospitalized COVID-19 patients found that higher serum IL-6 levels at admission were strong, independent predictors of worse survival and remained independently associated with disease severity and death after adjustment for multiple confounders [1]. Therefore, elevated IL-6 is associated with increased mortality risk in COVID-19 patients [1]. However, although disease severity commonly encompasses respiratory failure, this document does not explicitly report an association between IL-6 levels and respiratory failure specifically [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Severe COVID-19 can present with hyperinflammation resembling cytokine release syndrome, for which interleukin-6 (IL-6) blockade is an approved therapy, providing a rationale for using IL-6–blocking drugs such as tocilizumab in this setting [1]. In a single-center cohort of mechanically ventilated COVID-19 patients, tocilizumab—an IL-6 pathway blocker—was evaluated with survival post-intubation as the primary endpoint using inverse probability–weighted multivariable models [1]. In adjusted analyses, tocilizumab was associated with a 45% reduction in the hazard of death (hazard ratio 0.55, 95% CI 0.33–0.90), indicating a decreased likelihood of death with IL-6 blockade in this cohort [1]. Tocilizumab was also associated with improved status on an ordinal illness severity scale that incorporated superinfections (odds ratio per 1-level increase 0.59, 95% CI 0.36–0.95) [1]. Although superinfections were more frequent with tocilizumab (54% vs 26%; p<0.001), the 28-day case fatality rate among tocilizumab-treated patients did not differ between those with and without superinfection (22% vs 15%; p=0.42) [1]. The investigators interpreted these findings as an association between IL-6 blockade and decreased likelihood of death despite higher superinfection occurrence in this cohort [1]. Because this was an observational study, the authors emphasized that randomized controlled trials are needed to confirm whether IL-6–blocking drugs causally reduce mortality [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "The provided evidence shows that tocilizumab (Actemra) was associated with a lower risk of death and ICU admission or death in hospitalized COVID-19 patients with high C-reactive protein (CRP) levels (>150 mg/L), not with high interleukin-6 (IL-6) levels [1]. In adjusted marginal structural models, the association was significant only among patients with baseline CRP >150 mg/L (aHR for death 0.34, 95% CI 0.16–0.72; aHR for ICU admission or death 0.38, 95% CI 0.19–0.81) [1]. The study did not evaluate outcomes stratified by IL-6 concentrations, so it does not establish whether benefit varies by high IL-6 [1]. Therefore, based on the provided context, the claim that Actemra lowers the risk of death specifically in COVID-19 patients with high IL-6 is not supported [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "Evidence indicates that hyperinflammatory responses are a major cause of COVID-19 severity and death, implicating immune dysregulation rather than a single specific mechanism [1]. Macrophages are key innate immune cells that produce inflammatory mediators, and their dysregulated activation can be pathological, contributing to severe disease states including macrophage activation syndromes in related infections [1]. During SARS-CoV-2 infection, macrophages may play potentially pathological roles, and strategies to modulate macrophage activation are being explored therapeutically, indicating an important contributory role [1]. Therefore, COVID-19 severity cannot be attributed specifically and exclusively to macrophage activation, although macrophage-driven hyperinflammation is an important contributor [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "In symptomatic children and adults with acute respiratory illness, viral RNA from seasonal human coronaviruses, influenza viruses, and rhinoviruses was detectable in exhaled breath and coughs. [1] Surgical face masks significantly reduced detection of influenza virus RNA in respiratory droplets and coronavirus RNA in aerosols, demonstrating effective trapping of expelled viral material from coughs and breathing. [1] There was also a trend toward reduced detection of coronavirus RNA in respiratory droplets, further supporting mask effectiveness at capturing virus-laden droplets. [1] The study did not directly assess sneezes, so while the evidence supports effectiveness for coughs and exhaled breath, it does not specifically confirm effectiveness for sneezes in this dataset. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Evidence from surveillance in Yunnan identified two novel coronaviruses (RsSHC014 and Rs3367) in Chinese horseshoe bats that are more closely related to SARS-CoV than previously known bat viruses, particularly in the spike receptor-binding domain [1]. Researchers also isolated a live SARS-like coronavirus (WIV1) from bat feces that showed 99.9% sequence identity to Rs3367 and could use human ACE2 for cell entry, indicating the capacity to infect humans [1]. These findings provide the strongest evidence to date that Chinese horseshoe bats are natural reservoirs of SARS-CoV and suggest that intermediate hosts may not be required for some bat SARS-like coronaviruses to infect humans [1]. Therefore, the available data indicate that horseshoe bats in Yunnan are the most likely origin of the SARS-CoV that infected humans [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "A study modeling COVID-19 infectiousness found that viral load is highest at symptom onset and infectiousness peaks on or before symptom onset, indicating notable presymptomatic transmission but not necessarily that most transmission occurs before symptoms [1]. The study estimated that 44% (95% CI, 25–69%) of secondary cases were infected during the presymptomatic stage, which is substantial but below a majority based on the point estimate [1]. While the confidence interval allows for the possibility that presymptomatic transmission could exceed 50% in some settings, the central estimate does not support the claim that most transmission occurs before symptoms [1]. Consequently, presymptomatic transmission is important for control measures, but this evidence alone does not establish that most transmission occurs prior to symptom onset [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "no",
  "explanation": "In a large New York City cohort, high BMI (BMI>40) was a strong risk factor for being hospitalized with COVID-19 (OR 6.2), alongside advanced age and heart failure [1]. For critical illness (intensive care, mechanical ventilation, hospice and/or death), the strongest risk factors were admission oxygen saturation <88% (OR 6.99), d-dimer >2500 (OR 6.9), ferritin >2500 (OR 6.9), and CRP >200 (OR 5.78), not high BMI [1]. Decision-tree analyses showed age >65 and obesity were most important for predicting admission, whereas for critical illness the key splitters were low SpO2, elevated procalcitonin, lower troponin (protective), older age, and high CRP, indicating obesity was not among the highest-risk factors for critical illness [1]. Therefore, the claim that high BMI is one of the highest risk factors for critical COVID-19 hospitalization is not supported by this evidence [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "In a retrospective study of 112 hospitalized COVID-19 patients with cardiovascular disease in Wuhan, those classified as critically ill had a significantly higher median BMI than general cases (25.5 vs 22.0 kg/m^2; P=0.003), indicating an association between higher BMI and critical illness [1]. Among non-survivors, 88.24% had BMI >25 kg/m^2 compared with 18.95% of survivors, further linking higher BMI with worse outcomes and severity [1]. These data support that COVID-19 patients with higher BMI are more likely to become critically ill, at least within the studied population with cardiovascular comorbidities [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "In U.S. data from February 12–March 16, 2020, 45% of COVID-19 hospitalizations occurred among adults aged ≥65 years, which implies that the remaining 55% were among adults <65 years, indicating that young and middle-aged adults can require hospitalization. [1] Although severe outcomes were more common in older adults, the substantial share of hospitalizations in those under 65 shows that younger and middle-aged adults sometimes need inpatient care. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes; the study reports that anti-vaccination clusters are smaller in overall size than the pro-vaccination side of the Facebook network [1]. At the same time, these smaller anti-vaccination clusters are highly entangled with undecided clusters, while pro-vaccination clusters sit more peripherally in the network [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "In the cited retrospective analysis of 368 hospitalized COVID-19 patients in the U.S. Veterans Health Administration, hydroxychloroquine alone was associated with increased overall mortality compared with no hydroxychloroquine [1]. The adjusted hazard ratio for death for hydroxychloroquine alone versus no hydroxychloroquine was 2.61 (95% CI, 1.10–6.17; P=0.03), indicating a statistically significant higher risk of death [1]. In contrast, hydroxychloroquine combined with azithromycin was not associated with increased mortality (adjusted hazard ratio 1.14; 95% CI, 0.56–2.32; P=0.72) [1]. The authors concluded that while hydroxychloroquine (with or without azithromycin) did not reduce the risk of mechanical ventilation, an association of increased overall mortality was identified with hydroxychloroquine alone and emphasized the need for randomized controlled trials [1]."
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "The study reported no evidence that hydroxychloroquine, with or without azithromycin, reduced the risk of mechanical ventilation among hospitalized Covid-19 patients. [1] The adjusted hazard ratios for ventilation were 1.43 (95% CI, 0.53–3.79; P=0.48) for hydroxychloroquine alone and 0.43 (95% CI, 0.16–1.12; P=0.09) for hydroxychloroquine plus azithromycin versus no hydroxychloroquine, indicating no statistically significant reduction in the need for mechanical ventilation. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "Yes—according to a comparative observational study of hospitalized COVID-19 patients requiring oxygen, hydroxychloroquine did not reduce mortality risk. [1] Overall survival at day 21 was 89% in the hydroxychloroquine group versus 91% in the control group, with an adjusted estimate of 1.2 (95% CI 0.4–3.3), indicating no mortality benefit. [1] The study compared 84 patients treated within 48 hours of admission to 89 controls and adjusted for confounding using inverse probability of treatment weighting. [1] The authors concluded that these results do not support hydroxychloroquine use in hospitalized patients requiring oxygen. [1] Additionally, 10% of treated patients had electrocardiographic changes requiring discontinuation, underscoring potential safety concerns without mortality benefit. [1]"
}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "A multinational registry analysis of hospitalized COVID-19 patients found that those treated with hydroxychloroquine had higher rates of de-novo ventricular arrhythmias compared with patients who did not receive these drugs [1]. In this study, the control group had a 0.3% incidence of de-novo ventricular arrhythmia versus 6.1% in the hydroxychloroquine group (hazard ratio 2.369, 95% CI 1.935–2.900) [1]. The combination of hydroxychloroquine with a macrolide was associated with an 8.1% incidence of de-novo ventricular arrhythmia compared with 0.3% in controls (hazard ratio 5.106, 95% CI 4.106–5.983) [1]. The authors concluded that hydroxychloroquine, alone or with a macrolide, was associated with an increased frequency of ventricular arrhythmias when used to treat COVID-19 [1]."
}

